WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:06.840
With every rocket

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.842
we launch into the Earth's orbit,

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:12.078
we're trapping ourselves on our own planet.

00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:24.724
This is WHAT IF,

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:25.859
and here's what would happen

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:28.428
if we trashed Earth's orbit with space junk.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:32.799
Almost everything that
we've launched into space

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:36.131
ends up either falling down
and burning up in the atmosphere,

00:00:36.131 --> 00:00:40.435
or getting caught in the Earth's
lower orbit for thousands of years.

00:00:40.435 --> 00:00:42.375
The Earth’s lower orbit is surrounded

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:45.278
by just 200 kg (440 lbs)
of small, rocky debris

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:47.247
in the form of meteoroids.

00:00:47.247 --> 00:00:49.182
But it’s also got a belt

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:52.252
of about 3 million kg (6.6
million lbs) of space junk.

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:58.324
20,000 pieces of debris as large as softball,

00:00:58.324 --> 00:01:01.491
500,000 pieces larger than a marble

00:01:01.491 --> 00:01:04.898
and many millions of pieces
of debris too small to track

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:06.332
are orbiting the Earth.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.969
What makes these floating
parts of old satellites

00:01:09.969 --> 00:01:12.305
and spent rocket bodies so dangerous?

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:15.175
It's the speed they move at.

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:20.613
This junk is hurtling through space
at a speed of 8 km/s (5 mi/s).

00:01:20.613 --> 00:01:22.715
At this speed, one small bolt

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:24.851
is enough to shatter a working satellite

00:01:24.851 --> 00:01:27.087
into hundreds of pieces.

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:30.790
But it's what comes next that
could hold off our dreams

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:32.192
of building Moon bases,

00:01:32.192 --> 00:01:33.593
colonizing Mars,

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:36.755
and any space exploration whatsoever.

00:01:36.755 --> 00:01:40.432
The more junk we leave uncontrolled
in the Earth's lower orbit,

00:01:40.432 --> 00:01:43.009
the harder it is not to get hit by it.

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:46.306
Things are running into each other.

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:48.875
Fragments of debris are
colliding and breaking up,

00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:50.376
multiplying the number of items

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:52.738
flying through the space junkyard.

00:01:52.738 --> 00:01:56.116
And they're hitting working spacecraft, too.

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:58.251
One day they could hit enough satellites

00:01:58.251 --> 00:02:02.122
to initiate an unstoppable,
destructive chain reaction.

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:07.227
The cascade of collisions
would make our lower orbit

00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:09.462
so congested with man-made debris,

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:13.366
that eventually there would
be no active spacecraft left.

00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:15.668
Everything in the Earth's
orbit would be turned into

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:18.471
a deadly wall of celestial scrap. 

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:21.107
Near-Earth space would become unusable.

00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:22.942
No rockets could be safely launched

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:24.978
until we cleaned up the orbit.

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:27.313
We'd have to put our
space missions on pause,

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:30.984
and we'd be trapped on
the planet for generations.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.723
Well, the debris belt
wouldn't rain down on Earth

00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:38.658
and cause massive destruction.

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:41.027
Space rubbish would
disintegrate in the atmosphere

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:43.393
before it reached our planet's surface.

00:02:43.393 --> 00:02:46.533
But the collisions would produce a lot of dust.

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:49.279
That dust, illuminated by sunlight,

00:02:49.279 --> 00:02:52.405
would cause an ever-present
twilight on the planet.

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:54.140
With this form of light pollution,

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.075
you'd forget what nights used to look like.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.912
What's even more unpleasant -

00:02:58.912 --> 00:03:01.481
all of our satellite networks would go down.

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:04.083
There'd be no satellite communications,

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:05.660
no GPS navigation,

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:06.920
no weather data,

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.923
and no way to do any
science in the Earth's orbit.  

00:03:09.923 --> 00:03:11.925
You'd have to go back to paper maps,

00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:14.260
and get your cash out.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.429
Welcome to the 1970s.

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:19.866
On a positive note, science has
already come up with a few ideas

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:22.835
for cleaning up our lower orbit mess.

00:03:22.835 --> 00:03:25.638
They're looking at capturing
space debris with a net,

00:03:25.638 --> 00:03:27.340
or harpoon,

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:31.778
or vaporizing the small
bits of junk with lasers.

00:03:31.778 --> 00:03:33.580
Whatever action we deploy,

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.882
we should act quick if we
want to launch humanity

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:36.882
on far-space missions

00:03:36.882 --> 00:03:40.053
and finally colonize that red planet.

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:43.356
But that's a story for another WHAT IF.

